referenceSolutions.python:
def floorRegisterThresholdFile(root, k):
    a=root
    out=[]
    from collections import deque
    queue=deque([a] if a else [])
    while queue:
        s=0
        for _ in range(len(queue)):
            node=queue.popleft()
            s+=node.val
            if node.left: queue.append(node.left)
            if node.right: queue.append(node.right)
        out.append(s)
    return sum(x>=k for x in out)

referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int floorRegisterThresholdFile(TreeNode root, int k) {
    TreeNode a=root; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {if(a!=null){Deque<TreeNode>queue=new ArrayDeque<>();queue.add(a);while(!queue.isEmpty()){int count=queue.size(),s=0;while(count-->0){TreeNode node=queue.remove();s+=node.val;if(node.left!=null)queue.add(node.left);if(node.right!=null)queue.add(node.right);}out.add(s);}}}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)if(x>=k)answer++;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

root is the top node of a binary tree. Each node has a value and up to two children. The root is at depth 0, its children are at depth 1, and so on. Add the values at each depth. Put one sum per depth in the result list, starting at depth 0. Missing children add nothing. An empty tree gives an empty result list.

Return the answer

Count the numbers in the result list that are at least k (greater than or equal to k). Return that count. An empty list gives 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the number of nodes. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

